All 12 test cases pass (69 assertions).

**Implementation** (`binary_search_tree.h` — everything is a template, so it lives in the header):

```cpp
template<typename T>
class binary_tree {
public:
    explicit binary_tree(const T& data_value);
    void insert(const T& value);          // iterative: <= goes left, > goes right
    const T& data() const;
    const std::unique_ptr<binary_tree>& left() const;   // works with (bool) and ->
    const std::unique_ptr<binary_tree>& right() const;
    const_iterator begin() const;         // in-order
    const_iterator end() const;           // empty sentinel
private:
    T data_;
    std::unique_ptr<binary_tree> left_, right_;
};
```

Key points:
- **`insert`** walks down from the root, going left for values `<= node->data_` (so duplicates land on the left, as the `same_number_at_left_node` test expects) and right otherwise, allocating the child when it hits `nullptr`.
- **`const_iterator`** is a forward iterator holding a `std::stack<const binary_tree*>` of the pending "left spine". Construction pushes the whole left spine; `operator++` pops the top (next-smallest) and pushes the right child's left spine — giving in-order (sorted) traversal without recursion or extra storage. `end()` is the empty-stack iterator, so equality is just stack comparison.
- `binary_search_tree.cpp` stays as-is (no non-template code needed), and no names were changed.
